  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "If the $16$th term of an arithmetic series is $-32$ and the sum of the first $20$ terms is $-200,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=20$ and ${S}_{N}=-200$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -200 & =\\frac{20}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(20-1)d) \\\\ -200 & =10(2{a}_{1}+19d) \\\\ -20 & =2{a}_{1}+19d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $16$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{16} & ={a}_{1}+(16-1)d \\\\ -32 & ={a}_{1}+15d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-32={a}_{1}+15d \\\\ -20=2{a}_{1}+19d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =-2{a}_{1}-30d \\\\ -20 & =2{a}_{1}+19d \\\\ 44 & =-11d \\\\ d & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=-4$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32 & ={a}_{1}+15d \\\\ -32 & ={a}_{1}+15(-4) \\\\ -32 & ={a}_{1}-60 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
